 To organize and analyze Earth's magnetic field data in Java, you would first need to import the data into a Java program as an array or a list. Here is an example of how you can create a Java class to represent each data point and then store the data points in a list:
```java
public class MagneticFieldData {
    String location;
    double latitude;
    double longitude;
    double magneticFieldIntensity;
    public MagneticFieldData(String location, double latitude, double longitude, double magneticFieldIntensity) {
        this.location = location;
        this.latitude = latitude;
        this.longitude = longitude;
        this.magneticFieldIntensity = magneticFieldIntensity;
    }
}
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<MagneticFieldData> magneticFieldDataList = new ArrayList<>();
        magneticFieldDataList.add(new MagneticFieldData("A", 20.5, 50.2, 0.22));
        magneticFieldDataList.add(new MagneticFieldData("B", 40.2, 60.7, 0.18));
        magneticFieldDataList.add(new MagneticFieldData("C", 10.1, 70.5, 0.25));
        magneticFieldDataList.add(new MagneticFieldData("D", 30.7, 80.1, 0.19));
        magneticFieldDataList.add(new MagneticFieldData("E", 50.6, 90.8, 0.21));

        // Analyze the data here, for example by calculating the average magnetic field intensity
        double totalIntensity = 0.0;
        for (MagneticFieldData data : magneticFieldDataList) {
            totalIntensity += data.magneticFieldIntensity;
        }
        double averageIntensity = totalIntensity / magneticFieldDataList.size();
        System.out.println("The average magnetic field intensity is " + averageIntensity + " Tesla.");
    }
}
```

