 Here's a JavaScript function that takes an object as its argument and returns an array of all keys leading to the matched value when using dot notation, given a string key:

```javascript
function findNestedKey(obj, strKey) {
  let current = obj;
  const keys = strKey.split('.');
  const result = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < keys.length; i++) {
    if (current.hasOwnProperty(keys[i])) {
      current = current[keys[i]];
      result.unshift(keys[i]);
    } else {
      throw new Error(`No such property "${strKey}" on the object.`);
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

This function works by splitting the input string into an array of keys using the '.' character as the delimiter, then iteratively traverses the object using these keys until it finds the matching value or throws an error if the key is not present. The function keeps track of the keys visited so far and returns them as an array in reverse order when it reaches the target value. This allows the use of dot notation when accessing the final value.

