 To create a temperature converter component using React Hooks, I'll provide you with an example of a simple component that can convert temperatures between Celsius and Fahrenheit. This component will be named `TemperatureConverter`.

First, let's set up the basic structure of our component:

```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const TemperatureConverter = () => {
  // Set up state for input temperature in Celsius and converted temperature in Fahrenheit
  const [celsius, setCelsius] = useState(0);
  const [fahrenheit, setFahrenheit] = useState(32);

  // Function to handle conversion between Celsius and Fahrenheit
  const handleConvert = () => {
    setFahrenheit((prevFahrenheit) => {
      const newFahrenheit = (celsius * 9 / 5) + 32;
      return newFahrenheit;
    });
    setCelsius((prevCelsius) => prevCelsius); // Keep Celsius unchanged
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Temperature Converter</h1>
      <label htmlFor="celsiusInput">Enter temperature in Celsius:</label>
      <input
        type="number"
        id="celsiusInput"
        value={celsius}
        onChange={(e) => setCelsius(parseFloat(e.target.value))}
      />
      <button onClick={handleConvert}>Convert to Fahrenheit</button>
      <p>
        {fahrenheit}°F ({Math.round(fahrenheit)}°F when rounded)
      </p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default TemperatureConverter;
```

In this example, we use the `useState` hook to manage two states: `celsius` and `fahrenheit`. When the "Convert to Fahrenheit" button is clicked, the `handleConvert` function is called, which calculates the Fahrenheit equivalent of the given Celsius temperature and updates the state accordingly.

