 To retrieve the username from a JSON Web Token (JWT) in an ASP.NET C# WebApi project, you can follow these steps:

1. First, make sure that your JWT contains the claim for the username. In most cases, this claim is included under "sub" or "name" key. You can add it during token generation on the server side.

2. In your API controller action method, read the JWT token from the Authorization header using `System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt` package. If you haven't installed it yet, run `Install-Package Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens` in NuGet Package Manager Console.

3. Create a helper method to extract the username from the JWT token. Here's how you can implement it:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

public static string GetUsernameFromJwtToken(string jwtToken)
{
    try
    {
        var handler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
        var jsonToken = handler.ReadJwtToken(jwtToken);
        if (jsonToken == null) return null;

        // Assuming the username is under 'sub' or 'name' claim
        return jsonToken.Claims.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Type == "sub")?.Value;
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        throw new Exception("Invalid or expired JWT token", ex);
    }
}
```

