 Three pivotal moments during World War I and their consequences:

1. The Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand (June 28, 1914, Sarajevo, Bosnia): This event is considered the spark that ignited World War I. Gavrilo Princip, a member of the Serbian nationalist group known as the Black Hand, assassinated Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria-Hungary and his wife, Sophie, in Sarajevo. The assassination led Austria-Hungary to declare war on Serbia, which set off a chain reaction of alliances and conflicts among European powers. One lesser-known factor contributing to the outbreak of war was the complex system of alliances and treaties among European nations, which escalated the conflict quickly.

2. The Battle of the Marne (September 6-12, 1914, Marne River, France): This battle marked the end of the German advance into France and the beginning of trench warfare. The Germans had planned to outmaneuver the French and British armies and reach Paris before they could mobilize effectively. However, the Allied forces were able to rally and stop the German advance at the Marne River. One lesser-known factor contributing to the outcome of the battle was the use of taxis to transport French troops to the front lines more quickly than expected.

3. The United States' Entry into the War (April 6, 1917): The United States initially remained neutral in the conflict but eventually entered the war after Germany resumed unrestricted submarine warfare against American ships. The entry of the United States provided a significant boost to the Allied war effort due to its vast resources and manpower. One lesser-known factor contributing to America's entry into the war was the Zimmermann Telegram, a message from Germany to Mexico proposing an alliance against the United States if it remained neutral. The interception and decoding of this telegram by British intelligence helped sway public opinion in favor of intervention.

